.TH SYNCTANG\-KEYHOLDER 1 "" "synctang" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
synctang\-keyholder \- the laptop key holder for synctang
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B synctang\-keyholder init
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder export\-pubkey
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder unlock
.RI [ flags ]
.I machine\-transport\-id
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder status
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B synctang\-keyholder completion
.I shell
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B synctang\-keyholder
holds a long-term private key and answers a machine's recovery attempts on
demand.
The machine runs
.BR synctang\-unlocker (8)
and waits at its LUKS prompt; the key holder always dials, never listens.
It looks the machine up by its transport identity, shows the machine's name and
identity, waits for a person to approve, answers once, and reports how it went.
.PP
The private key stays on this computer.
The machine recovers the volume secret from the answer, checks that it opens
the volume, and only then uses it.
By default the public Syncthing relay and discovery servers are used, and on the
same local network the machine is found by multicast; see
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5)
to use your own discovery server.
.SH COMMANDS
Every command accepts
.B \-h
and prints its flags and exits with status 2.
Flags may be written with one or two dashes.
.SS init
Generates a new long-term key and this key holder's transport identity, and
writes them under
.IR <user\ config\ dir> /synctang\-keyholder/
(see
.BR FILES ).
It refuses to overwrite an existing key, because the volumes enrolled against it
could no longer be opened.
The transport identity is always kept if it already exists, even with
.BR \-\-force :
a new one would change this key holder's Device ID and break every machine that
authorises it.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
Where to write the long-term private key.
Default: the file
.I key
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This key holder's persistent transport identity.
Default: the file
.I transport.pem
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Replace an existing key, held by whichever store.
The old copy is removed from its store if the new key is kept in another, so that no replaced key
is left behind.
.TP
\fB\-\-store\fR \fIstore\fR
Where to keep the long-term key:
.BR file ,
.BR secret\-service ,
or
.B auto
(the default).
.B file
is a plain file with mode 0600: it keeps out other users of the laptop and nothing else.
.B secret\-service
is the desktop's keyring over D-Bus (GNOME Keyring, KWallet or KeePassXC), encrypted with a key that
the login unlocks, so a copy of the configuration directory is useless without the login password;
it is unlocked for the whole session, so software running as you can ask for it, and it needs a session
bus, which plain ssh does not give.
.B auto
is the keyring when there is one and a plain file, with a warning that says why, when there is not.
A store named explicitly is never replaced by another when it is not there: the command fails.
The choice is recorded beside the key file, in
.BR key.store ,
which holds no secret; the other commands follow it.
.BR status
says what holds the key.
.SS export\-pubkey
Prints the public key and the transport identity, to give to
.BR "synctang\-unlocker enrol" :
.PP
.nf
S: <hex public key>
transport\-id: <transport identity>
.fi
.PP
The transport identity is created if it does not exist yet.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
The long-term private key.
Default: the file
.I key
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This key holder's persistent transport identity.
Default: the file
.I transport.pem
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.SS unlock
Answers one machine's recovery attempt.
The argument is the machine's transport identity, as printed by
.B synctang\-unlocker enrol
or
.BR "synctang\-unlocker status" .
.PP
It prints the machine's name and identity and waits for Enter before
answering; Ctrl-C aborts.
The name is chosen by the machine and proves nothing, so compare the identity.
With
.B \-\-yes
the prompt is skipped.
It then answers once, waits to be told whether the machine could use the answer,
and prints
.B unlocked
only when it was.
If an attempt fails, another is made every two seconds until the timeout.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
The long-term private key.
Default: the file
.I key
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This key holder's persistent transport identity.
Default: the file
.I transport.pem
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip the approval prompt.
.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIduration\fR
Give up dialling the machine after this long.
Default 2 minutes.
.TP
\fB\-\-discovery\fR \fIURL\fR
The discovery server to look the machine up on, instead of the public one.
The machine must announce to the same one.
The URL may say how to trust the server, with
.B ?id=
or
.BR ?insecure ;
see
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5).
.TP
\fB\-\-discovery\-announce\fR \fIURL\fR
The discovery announce URL, if it differs from
.BR \-\-discovery .
Accepted so that the same two keys work here as on a machine; a key holder only
looks up and never announces, so it does not change how the machine is found.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIfile\fR
A file with the
.B discovery
and
.B discovery\-announce
settings, in the format of
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5).
Default: the file
.I config
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ),
which need not exist.
A file named here must exist.
A
.B relay\-pool
line is refused: only the machine joins a relay, and a key holder learns it from
discovery.
Flags win over the file.
.TP
\fB\-\-direct\-addr\fR \fIhost:port\fR
Dial this address directly, bypassing relay and discovery.
For testing only.
.SS status
Says where the long-term key is kept and what that does and does not protect against, in
plain words, and whether the key can be read: it loads the key, only to say so, and wipes it
at once.
It prints no secret, and changes nothing: it makes no key, no record and no transport identity.
When a plain copy of the key is still beside a key that another store holds, which is what a
migration leaves, it says so, because that copy undoes what the other store is for.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
Where the key is, or, when it is kept elsewhere, where its record is
.RB ( key.store
beside it).
Default: the file
.I key
in the key holder's directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR
This key holder's transport identity, whose Device ID is printed.
Default: the file
.I transport.pem
in the key holder's directory.
.SS completion
Writes to standard output the completion script for the shell named: bash, zsh or fish.
The program makes the script from its own usage text and the flag help of each command, so it
names exactly the commands and flags of this version and cannot fall out of step with them.
The Debian package installs the three scripts under
.BR /usr/share/bash\-completion/completions ,
.B /usr/share/zsh/vendor\-completions
and
.BR /usr/share/fish/vendor_completions.d .
Without the package, bash can load it from
.B ~/.bashrc
with
.BR "source <(synctang\-keyholder completion bash)" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded; for
.BR unlock ,
the machine reported that it used the answer.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed.
The reason is printed on standard error with the prefix
.BR synctang\-keyholder: .
For
.B unlock
this includes running out of time without a successful answer.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong: an unknown command or flag, a missing argument, or a
setting that is not valid.
.SH FILES
All under
.IR <user\ config\ dir> /synctang\-keyholder/ ,
which is
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/synctang\-keyholder/
if
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is set and
.B ~/.config/synctang\-keyholder/
otherwise.
.TP
.I <user\ config\ dir>/synctang\-keyholder/key
The long-term private key, hex-encoded.
Mode 0600, in a directory created with mode 0700.
Whoever can read it can answer for this key holder, so keep it private and back
it up: without it, the volumes enrolled against it cannot be opened.
.TP
.I <user\ config\ dir>/synctang\-keyholder/transport.pem
This key holder's transport identity, a certificate and key, whose Device ID the
machines authorise.
Mode 0600.
.TP
.I <user\ config\ dir>/synctang\-keyholder/config
Optional settings; see
.BR \-\-config .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the user config directory is.
Default
.BR ~/.config .
.SH EXAMPLES
Create the key and read off what the machine needs:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-keyholder init
synctang\-keyholder export\-pubkey
.fi
.PP
Unlock a machine:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-keyholder unlock <machine transport id>
.fi
.PP
Unlock through a private discovery server that has a self-signed certificate,
named by its Device ID:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-keyholder unlock \e
  \-\-discovery 'https://192.0.2.20:8443/v2/?id=<server Device ID>' \e
  <machine transport id>
.fi
.PP
Rotate the key and keep the transport identity, then enrol the new key:
.PP
.nf
synctang\-keyholder init \-\-force
synctang\-keyholder export\-pubkey
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR synctang\-unlocker (8),
.BR synctang\-network.conf (5)
